<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seo Hui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/942">942</a>–<a href="/years/998">998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seo Hui was a Korean statesman and diplomat of the Goryeo period. In 993, when a Khitan army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> invaded, he negotiated with its commander and secured its withdrawal. Goryeo accepted Liao suzerainty but gained the land east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a>, where six garrison districts were built.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sŏ Hŭi belonged to the Icheon Sŏ clan, which until the time of his grandfather Sŏ Sin-il had been a hojok, a powerful local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> clan of the Icheon area in the south-east of Gyeonggi Province. His father, Sŏ P'il, served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwangjong-of-goryeo">King Gwangjong</a> as Secretary-General, the highest post of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Royal Secretariat. His sons also rose high: Sŏ Nul became Munhasijung, the highest minister of state, and one of Sŏ Nul's daughters married King Hyeonjong, making the clan maternal relatives of the king. His art name was Yŏmyun and his posthumous name Changwi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sŏ Hŭi passed the state examination, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwageo">gwageo</a>, with a high grade in March 960, the 11th year of Gwangjong's reign. In 972 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and helped to restore relations between Goryeo and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, broken off more than a decade earlier. In 983 he took charge of military affairs, and he later rose to the second rank of the Chancellory and finally to Taebo Naesaryeong, head of the Supreme Council.</p><p>In 993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> general Xiao Sunning captured Pongsan county, drove Goryeo's forces behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a> and demanded surrender. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seongjong-of-goryeo">Seongjong of Goryeo</a> at first planned to give up the land north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, but Sŏ Hŭi argued that the Liao were acting from fear and urged one more battle. After Xiao was checked at the Battle of Anyung Fortress, Sŏ went to the Liao camp. When Xiao claimed the former Koguryŏ lands for Liao, Sŏ answered that Goryeo was itself former Koguryŏ, as its name and its capital at P'yŏngyang showed, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> had taken the land on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a>; once they were driven out and the roads opened, he said, Goryeo could hardly refuse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sŏ reported to his king an agreement with Xiao to drive out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> jointly and seize their land, giving Goryeo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> a closer land border and matching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a>. He then fortified the newly gained territory, which gave Goryeo a decisive victory in the second and third conflicts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> after his death. He died on the 14th day of the 7th lunar month of 998, 8 August 998. He is remembered for diplomacy that led 60,000 Khitan troops to leave Goryeo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sŏ Hŭi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 993 a Khitan army invaded, but the official <b>Seo Hui</b> negotiated its withdrawal and secured the land east of the Yalu that became six garrison districts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,126.55&amp;year=978" aria-label="Kaesong on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7704 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Seo Hui"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8154 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8154 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,126.55&amp;year=978">What was here in 978 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
